WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.758
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Last time, we
created a Kubernetes cluster

00:00:02.758 --> 00:00:05.310
and set up the new Stackdriver
monitoring and logging on it.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.140
Today, we're going
to set up alerting

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.598
to let us know if there are
any issues with our application

00:00:09.598 --> 00:00:10.600
running on that cluster.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.510
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.450
What can we do to
get an early heads up

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.260
that something is going wrong?

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.380
Well, the first thing
to do is to figure out

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.320
what our service level
indicators, or SLIs,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.330
should be, and then
have Stackdriver

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.700
let us know if and when our
application may be in danger

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.150
of violating the Service
Level Objectives, or SLOs,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.650
we'll define.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.330
You may be thinking-- wait, SLI?

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.410
What's that?

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.720
Well, an SLI is the metric
we'll measure and use

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.940
as a proxy for user happiness.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.500
For our service running
on our GKE cluster,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.500
we're primarily going to
care about two things--

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.570
latency and availability.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:51.490
We'll want to know if
and when either of those

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.500
drops below our
desired thresholds.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.540
But how?

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:57.340
The great news is that
Stackdriver gives us

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.550
an easy way to do what we
want with alerting policies.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.725
Let's set one up for
availability now.

00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:06.940
But first, what should
we use as our SLI?

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.960
The first option is
pretty straightforward.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.630
We simply have the
application output

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.370
all errors to standard
out or standard

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.980
error, which will then show
up in Stackdriver logging

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.280
automatically.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.100
We can then configure
log-based metrics

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.800
from those that'll tell us
how many errors or failures

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.250
we're seeing, and we
can use that as our SLI.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:27.310
Another option would be to use a
proper instrumentation approach

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.140
with something like OpenCensus.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.750
We won't get into the
details of that library here,

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.180
but you can watch Morgan
do a great deep dive on it.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.250
We'll post a link to
that in the description.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.388
Our third option to
consider is Prometheus.

00:01:38.388 --> 00:01:40.180
It's a leading open
source monitoring tool,

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.100
and the good news is
that the beta version

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.530
of Stackdriver monitoring
for Kubernetes supports it.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.510
Just install Prometheus
into your cluster,

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:47.710
and you're good to go.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.510
Export metrics in
your application,

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.520
and the Prometheus exporter
will ship them right

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.260
into the Stackdriver backend.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.330
So those are our three options--

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.172
log metrics, OpenCensus,
and Prometheus.

00:01:58.172 --> 00:01:59.630
No matter which
one you choose, you

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.230
will have metrics you can use
to represent availability.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.000
Let's do that now.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.060
We can create an alerting
policy using the GRPC completed

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.720
RPCs metric to let us know when
we get more failures than we

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.938
allocate an error budget
for, and we're done.

00:02:13.938 --> 00:02:16.480
I should note that, while error
rate is a great service level

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.020
indicator, this alerting policy
is not a true service level

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.035
objective.

00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:22.660
It doesn't have a
good way to represent

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.820
what percentage of
overall calls are failing,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.410
which is what we really need.

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.900
That is provided by the service
monitoring functionality

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.380
in Stackdriver, which we'll
cover in a future video.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.210
Now that we've got
availability covered,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.632
let's take care of
our latency SLI.

00:02:36.632 --> 00:02:38.590
As with availability,
there's more than one way

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.630
to measure application latency.

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.810
We can use load balancer logs,
an instrumentation library

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.690
like OpenCensus, or even logging
from the application itself.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.920
As before, let's start by
creating an alerting policy.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.160
We'll select an OpenCensus
latency metric as our condition

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.620
and put a response
time threshold on it.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:57.980
And there we go--

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.190
we've now got our application
running in Kubernetes

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.260
and instrument for monitoring.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.040
And Stackdriver's
going to tell us

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.830
if anything goes
wrong that impacts

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:07.300
latency or availability.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.580
When something happens that
causes our availability

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.240
or latency to fall below the
thresholds we've defined,

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.410
Stackdriver will
create an incident.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.050
That incident can also be
used to trigger notifications

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.940
like email messages,
integrations

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.510
with other alert management
systems, or even web hook

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.250
calls to trigger remediation
actions if we want.

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.410
When the application is
restored to a healthy state

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.530
through some corrective
actions, like rolling back

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.720
a breaking change, the
incident will be resolved.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.520
Come back next time,
when we take a deeper

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.400
look at troubleshooting latency
issues with Stackdriver Trace.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.930
That's it for this episode.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:39.400
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.390
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

